Saved gateway connection-test resource authority
================================================

A saved gateway test checks the currently active organization and, when the
connection is assigned to a site, that site's organization, active status and
soft-delete state before contacting the device. Existing organization and
site-grant admission checks remain. Unassigned gateways remain supported for
callers whose existing access allows them.

After device I/O ends, the service reloads the original gateway scope with
populate_existing and row locks on the gateway, organization and optional site.
The locks remain until the request transaction commits or rolls back. Lost or
inactive resource scope returns HTTP 404 without the old test metadata. Changed
connection inputs, encrypted credentials, settings, revision or status/metadata
return HTTP 409 with a fixed instruction to retry. The obsolete result is not
persisted or returned. A concurrent edit or newer check therefore wins.

No resource lock spans device I/O. The existing request database transaction
still remains open during that I/O; this change does not introduce a separate
request transaction lifecycle. Adapter cleanup completes before result locking.
Explicit certificate-verification overrides and unchanged successful/failed
outcomes retain their existing semantics without rewriting saved trust/settings.

The qualification uses actual encrypted PostgreSQL rows, HTTP routes and
independently committed writers. Separate-process checks exercise concurrent
mutations and NOWAIT lock contention, including release after commit. The
request's authenticated identity and adapter results are synthetic. This is a
database/HTTP authority proof, not a vendor transport or hardware qualification.
Existing related HTTP/TLS tests remain regression coverage for the clients.

The resource guard does not reload caller sessions, API keys, roles or site-grant
records after I/O. It cannot recall credentials already sent or cancel device
I/O immediately when authority changes, and it does not undo external device
operations. Other gateway live/sync/write paths and arbitrary provider metadata
or other logs require their own review. No schema or dependency changes occur.
